    "interpretation": {
      "finding": "Same-support fitted predictions reduce large negative-utility errors but worsen errors for most empirically no-death candidates; MSE and MAE disagree because they weight error magnitudes differently.",
      "mechanism_supported_by_code_and_labels": "Utility training uses squared loss, whose population optimum is the conditional arithmetic mean. The candidate objective has defeat cost 1000, while 90.36% of valued validation candidates have sampled death target zero and their mean utility is -5.62. A shift toward the negative tail can lower MSE while moving away from typical labels. This explains the measured direction; it does not prove the fitted model is optimal or calibrated.",
      "measured_tail_vs_majority_tradeoff": {
        "zero_death_global_mae_change": 37.81662816692279,
        "mixed_death_global_mae_change": -7.970555300687021,
        "all_death_global_mae_change": -21.812675245250865,
        "zero_death_global_mse_change": 8790.084432895692,
        "mixed_death_global_mse_change": -7039.7302938031935,
        "all_death_global_mse_change": -34864.81008227893
      },
      "not_only_average_shift": "The fitted model beats the train-only constant on both validation MAE (99.79 vs 129.98) and MSE (39844.98 vs 64742.80); final mean predictions differ across sampled death groups. This is descriptive evidence of useful fitting beyond one global constant, not causal or policy-strength certification.",
      "remaining_bias": "For empirical death target 1, the final mean prediction is -357.65 versus mean target -1012.01. For target 0, mean prediction is -35.33 versus mean target -5.62. Tail severity remains underestimated and pessimistic errors appear among sampled no-death cases.",
      "noise_and_selection": "Every valued candidate in both parsed splits has 4 allocated worlds. Empirical death zero is not true zero risk. Same records, hashes and reconstructed metrics rule out population changes or reporter drift as the cause of the measured delta; finite-N label noise and source selection remain unestimated. All four marginal phase groups show worse MAE and better MSE, so phase reweighting did not create the sign reversal.",
      "potion_limit": "All 193 valued potion validation candidates belong to 40 partial roots and are play/end_turn actions with sampled death zero. Across 44 potion roots, all 44 potion and all 44 discard_potion actions have missing utility; no potion root supports complete-case ranking. The observed potion regression cannot establish performance on use/discard decisions.",
      "policy_limit": "On complete-value validation roots, 34 improve and 30 worsen in empirical regret, with 417 equal; category and phase results are heterogeneous. These are teacher-mean comparisons, not measured student rollouts or certified preferences."
    }
  },
